# Project source style, enforced by the "Format check" step in
# .github/workflows/tests.yml.
#
# BasedOnStyle: LLVM is deliberate and load-bearing. The already-formatted
# sources in this tree match the LLVM defaults exactly, so pinning them means
# a stray `clang-format -i` is a no-op instead of a surprise diff. Before this
# file existed there was no config and no check, so formatters ran freely and
# produced large mixed diffs that entangled unrelated work — and silently broke
# the tests that pin real source bodies by text (they are now token-tolerant).
#
# Do not add style keys that reflow more than LLVM already does: the tree is
# clean under exactly this config, and any extra key invalidates that.
#
# SCOPE: .m/.h/.c/.mm/.inc only. The .x hook sources are EXEMPT and must never
# be formatted: they are Logos-preprocessed, and clang-format has no Logos
# support -- it rewrites `%ctor {` to `% ctor {` and collapses
# `% group X % hook Y` onto one line, which does not compile. Any .x can gain
# Logos directives, so the exemption is by extension, not a per-file list.
# The format check in .github/workflows/tests.yml selects files accordingly.
#
# BreakStringLiterals: false is the one deliberate deviation from the LLVM
# defaults. At the default, clang-format re-wraps long literals into adjacent
# ones, which splits even short literals at an unlucky column -- `table:@"App"`
# became `@"Ap" @"p"`. That is valid C, but it mangles authored text and broke
# every gate that pins source by text. Turning it off keeps the tree clean
# under this config (verified: no diff) and stops the churn at the source.
BasedOnStyle: LLVM
BreakStringLiterals: false
